if (manager && currentRendererId === rendererId) {
    // A different renderer type (e.g., Server vs WebAssembly) is trying to enable JS root components.
    // This is a multi-host scenario which is not supported for dynamic root components.
    throw new Error('Dynamic root components have already been enabled.');
  }

// When the same renderer type re-enables (e.g., circuit restart or new circuit on same page),
  // accept the new manager. The old manager's DotNetObjectReference is no longer valid anyway
  // because the old circuit is gone. We don't dispose the old manager - doing so would cause
  // JSDisconnectedException because the circuit that created it no longer exists.
  currentRendererId = rendererId;
  manager = managerInstance;

[Theory]
[InlineData("ServerNonPrerendered")]
[InlineData("WebAssemblyNonPrerendered")]
public void PersistentStateIsSupportedInDynamicJSRoots(string renderMode)
{
    Navigate($"subdir/WasmMinimal/dynamic-js-root.html?renderMode={renderMode}");

    Browser.Equal("Counter", () => Browser.Exists(By.TagName("h1")).Text);
    Browser.Equal("Current count: 0", () => Browser.Exists(By.CssSelector("p[role='status']")).Text);

    Browser.Click(By.CssSelector("button.btn-primary"));
    Browser.Equal("Current count: 1", () => Browser.Exists(By.CssSelector("p[role='status']")).Text);
}

findings.body:
The guard in `enableJSRootComponents` is `if (manager && currentRendererId === rendererId)`. It throws when the renderer ID matches the stored one. That is the restart case the PR is supposed to accept.

Renderer IDs are fixed constants (`WebRendererId.Server = 1`, `WebAssembly = 2`, in `src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/WebRendererId.ts`). So after circuit 1 is enabled with `rendererId = 1`, circuit 2 calls `attachWebRendererInterop(WebRendererId.Server, ...)` from `CircuitManager.reconnect` with `rendererId = 1`. The condition is true, and the function throws. The PR description says the restart path does "No exception thrown", so the code contradicts the stated design.

::diff{file="src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/JSRootComponents.ts" lines="120-137"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The reverse condition is the intended one: throw only when a *different* renderer is trying to take over, and accept the same renderer.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/JSRootComponents.ts" lines="126-130"}
if (manager && currentRendererId !== undefined && currentRendererId !== rendererId) {
  // A different renderer type (Server vs WebAssembly) is enabling JS root components on the same page.
  throw new Error('Dynamic root components have already been enabled.');
}

The PR's only test change moves the existing `PersistentStateIsSupportedInDynamicJSRoots` test. It does not check the new behavior: a restart re-enabling with the same renderer, the no-throw path, or the cross-renderer throw. The inverted guard above went unnoticed for this reason. A single E2E test that navigates, forces a circuit restart, and confirms `Blazor.rootComponents.add` works would have caught it.

::code{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/Tests/StatePersistenceTest.cs" lines="279-292"}

The moved test only covers one circuit and one renderer.

Removing the `RegisterDynamicJSRootComponent` flag means `RazorComponentEndpointsStartup` always calls `RegisterForJavaScript` for `dynamic-js-root-counter`. Every app using this startup now has a non-empty `jsComponentParameters`, so `attachWebRendererInterop` runs `enableJSRootComponents` and the initializer path for every test in that fixture, not just the ones that need it. That broadens the behavior under test and makes failures harder to attribute.

The PR also removes the `Razor Component Endpoints with JS Root Component` entry from `Program.cs`. I did not find references to it, so this is safe, but the always-on registration is a shared-fixture change.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/testassets/Components.TestServer/RazorComponentEndpointsStartup.cs" lines="50-56"}
